<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Liliʻuokalani, a 78-year-old woman who had a minor ischemic stroke two months ago and takes apixaban for paroxysmal atrial fibrillation, came the same day with a nosebleed. This morning, after blowing her nose, the left nostril bled steadily for about 15 minutes; it stopped when a member of her ʻohana had her sit forward and pinch the soft part of her nose for ten minutes, as the clinic&apos;s handout showed. She did not swallow much blood or vomit. The air in her bedroom has been dry from a fan running all night, and her nose has felt crusted for a week. This is her first nosebleed in years. She has had no other bleeding: no bruising, blood in the urine, black stools or bleeding gums. She takes apixaban twice daily and has not missed a dose, and she has taken no aspirin, ibuprofen or naproxen. She has had no new weakness, numbness or speech trouble. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>This morning, after blowing her nose</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>Left nostril</td></tr><tr><td>Duration</td><td>About 15 minutes; stopped with ten minutes of firm pressure</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>Steady bleeding from the front of the nose; no blood down the throat</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Apixaban for four weeks; dry air from a fan at night; nasal crusting for a week</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>Leaning forward and firm pressure stopped it</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>No other bleeding, lightheadedness or new neurological symptoms</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

