  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>Transient weakness and clumsiness of the right hand with word-finding difficulty, lasting about 20 minutes and fully resolved, the afternoon before the visit; suspected transient ischemic attack, ABCD2 score 5 (high risk); NIH Stroke Scale 0</td><td>resolved</td><td>provisional</td>
    <td>G45.9</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting problem; resolved into the stroke diagnosis when diffusion MRI showed a small infarct at the stroke clinic the next day. Not a claim about her real symptoms.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Minor ischemic stroke: a small acute infarct in the cortex of the left precentral gyrus (left middle cerebral artery territory) on diffusion MRI, embolic in pattern, with full recovery (NIH Stroke Scale 0); source found to be paroxysmal atrial fibrillation; synthetic modern episode linked to her documented death from a stroke</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>I63.412</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis made at the stroke clinic the day after the visit; the 1917 cause of death remains D.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Hypertension: told of borderline readings in past years and not treated; 156/88 mmHg at intake</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>I10</td><td>S — Synthetic risk factor; no blood pressure from her lifetime is recorded in the cited sources.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-4">
    <td>Paroxysmal atrial fibrillation found on 30-day ambulatory monitoring after the stroke (six episodes, the longest 3 hours 10 minutes; burden about 3%); CHA2DS2-VASc 6, HAS-BLED 2</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>I48.0</td><td>S — Synthetic finding on prolonged rhythm monitoring at five weeks.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-5">
    <td>Anterior nosebleed from the left side of the nasal septum four weeks after starting apixaban, stopped with pressure; hemoglobin unchanged; resolved</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>R04.0</td><td>S — Synthetic intercurrent problem at an unscheduled visit; a common minor bleed on an anticoagulant, not a documented illness.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

