<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Anterior nosebleed on apixaban (R04.0)</content>. A minor anterior nosebleed from a dry, crusted septum, stopped with pressure; hemoglobin 12.5 g/dL, unchanged from 12.7 before apixaban. This is not a reason to stop anticoagulation: her stroke risk without it (CHA2DS2-VASc 6) far outweighs the risk from a minor bleed. Silver nitrate cautery of the vessel was offered; she chose to try moisturizing first, and that is reasonable while the bleeding has stopped. [S]<list><item>Petroleum jelly or saline gel inside the front of both nostrils twice daily, and a humidifier or a damp towel near the fan at night.</item><item>If it bleeds again: sit up, lean forward and pinch the soft part of the nose firmly for 10 to 15 minutes without letting go to check.</item><item>Go to the emergency department if it bleeds for more than 20 minutes despite pressure, runs down the throat, or makes her lightheaded.</item><item>Continue apixaban 5 mg twice daily without missing doses; avoid aspirin, ibuprofen and naproxen.</item><item>Cautery at the clinic, or an ear, nose and throat specialist, if it happens again.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Paroxysmal atrial fibrillation on apixaban (I48.0)</content>. Regular pulse today. HAS-BLED 2; no modifiable bleeding risks other than avoiding anti-inflammatory medicines. [S]<list><item>No change.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: 2023 ACC/AHA/ACCP/HRS Guideline for the Diagnosis and Management of Atrial Fibrillation (2023); Signs and Symptoms of Stroke (2026); Clinical Practice Guideline: Nosebleed (Epistaxis) (2020)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Nurse telephone call in three days. Three-month review in four weeks. Call 911 for any BE FAST sign. Next scheduled visit 1917-06-15: three-month review after the stroke: function, mood, blood pressure, apixaban safety and falls. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
